, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(-t)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+{(t+2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+4t+4) & =4 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4t+4 & =4 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-1$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-(-1)=1 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}(-1)=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=(-1)+2=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,-\\sqrt{2},1).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}(0)=0 \\\\ z=(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(0,0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70954" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-70954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,-2\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2)$"] ["text" "$(-2,-\\sqrt{2},2)$ and $(-1,0,\\sqrt{2})$"] ["text" "$(-\\sqrt{2},1,-1)$ and $(2,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(1,-\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2)$"] ["text" "$(1,\\sqrt{2},3)$ and $(1,2\\sqrt{2},2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70954" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,-\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=25$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=2t+5 \\\\ y=-2t, \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (2t+5)^{2}+(-2t)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3}t)^{2} & =25 \\\\ (4{t}^{2}+20t+25)+4{t}^{2}+12{t}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 20{t}^{2}+20t+25 & =25 \\\\ 20{t}^{2}+20t & =0 \\\\ 20t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+5=5 \\\\ y=-2(0)=0 \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(5,0,0).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(-1)+5=3 \\\\ y=-2(-1)=2 \\\\ z=2\\sqrt{3}(-1)=-2\\sqrt{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(1,-1,\\sqrt{3})$ only"] ["text" "$(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$ only"] ["text" "$(5,0,0)$ and $(1,-1,\\sqrt{3})$"] ["text" "$(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$"] ["text" "$(5,0,0)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71950" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(5,0,0)$ and $(3,2,-2\\sqrt{3})$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=1+t \\\\ y=t, \\\\ z=-t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+t)^{2}+6(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2(1+2t+{t}^{2})+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 2+4t+2{t}^{2}+6{t}^{2}+{t}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 9{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(9t+4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{4}{9}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ y=-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ z=-(-\\frac{4}{9})=\\frac{4}{9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9}).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+0=1 \\\\ y=0 \\\\ z=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,0,0).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(1,0,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(2,1,-1)$ and $(1,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(2,1,-1)$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71131" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(\\frac{5}{9},-\\frac{4}{9},\\frac{4}{9})$ and $(1,0,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8(t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+4(t)^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-1$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ z=(-1)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-1,-2,-1).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(1)=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=(1)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1,2,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1,2,1)$"] ["text" "$(-1,2,-1)$ and $(1,-2,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,-2,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(1,2,-1)$ and $(-1,-2,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,2,-1)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70568" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1,2,1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2}=2{z}^{2}$ and the line $\\frac{x}{3}=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=y+1=\\frac{z}{3}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=3t \\\\ y=t-1 \\\\ z=3t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+5{y}^{2} & =2{z}^{2} \\\\ 2(3t)^{2}+5(t-1)^{2} & =2(3t)^{2} \\\\ 5(t-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ t-1 & =0 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3(1)=3 \\\\ y=1-1=0 \\\\ z=3(1)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(3,0,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-3,-2,-3)$  and $(0,-1,0)$"] ["text" "$(-6,-3,-6)$ and $(3,0,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,-1,0)$ only"] ["text" "$(3,0,3)$ only"] ["text" "$(6,1,6)$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94785" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(3,0,3)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$ and the line $\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=6t \\\\ y=2t \\\\ z=2t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{15}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+3{z}^{2} & =15 \\\\ (6t)^{2}+3(2t)^{2}+3(2t)^{2} & =15 \\\\ 36{t}^{2}+12{t}^{2}+12{t}^{2} & =15 \\\\ 60{t}^{2} & =15 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6(-\\frac{1}{2})=-3 \\\\ y=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-3,-1,-1).$\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=6(\\frac{1}{2})=3 \\\\ y=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(3,1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-3,-1,-1)$ and $(3,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-3,-1,-1)$ and $(3,1,1)$"] ["text" "$(3,-1,1)$ and $(-3,1,-1)$"] ["text" "$(3,1,1)$ only"] ["text" "$(\\frac{3}{2},-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$"] ["text" "$(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71138" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(-3,-1,-1)$ and $(3,1,1)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=49$ and the line $\\frac{x-3}{2}=y+7=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{2}}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y+7}{1}=\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{2}}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+3 \\\\ y=t-7 \\\\ z=\\sqrt{2}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =49 \\\\ ((2t+3)-3)^{2}+(t-7)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} & =49 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+(t-7)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}t)^{2} & =49 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}-14t+49)+2{t}^{2} & =49 \\\\ 7{t}^{2}-14t+49 & =49 \\\\ 7{t}^{2}-14t & =0 \\\\ 7t(t-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=2.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+3=3 \\\\ y=(0)-7=-7 \\\\ z=\\sqrt{2}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(3,-7,0).$\n\n- For $t=2$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(2)+3=7 \\\\ y=(2)-7=-5 \\\\ z=\\sqrt{2}(2)=2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(-1,-9,-2\\sqrt{2})$"] ["text" "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(-1,9,2)$"] ["text" "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2})$"] ["text" "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,\\sqrt{2})$"] ["text" "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,5,2\\sqrt{2})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71955" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(3,-7,0)$ and $(7,-5,2\\sqrt{2})$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}+{z}^{2}=121$ and the line $\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+6}{6}=\\frac{z+11}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+6}{6}=\\frac{z+11}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=6t-6 \\\\ z=2t-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =121 \\\\ ((2t+2)-2)^{2}+((6t-6)+6)^{2}+(2t-11)^{2} & =121 \\\\ (2t)^{2}+(6t)^{2}+(2t-11)^{2} & =121 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+36{t}^{2}+(4{t}^{2}-44t+121) & =121 \\\\ 44{t}^{2}-44t+121 & =121 \\\\ 44{t}^{2}-44t & =0 \\\\ t(t-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ y=6(0)-6=-6 \\\\ z=2(0)-11=-11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(2,-6,-11).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)+2=4 \\\\ y=6(1)-6=0 \\\\ z=2(1)-11=-9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,0,-9).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(2,-6,-11)$ and $(4,0,-9).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(1,-6,-2)$ and $(9,0,-4)$"] ["text" "$(2,-11,-6)$ and $(4,9,0)$"] ["text" "$(2,-6,-11)$ and $(4,0,-9)$"] ["text" "$(2,6,-1)$ and $(-9,0,4)$"] ["text" "$(6,-2,-11)$ and $(0,4,-9)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-70961" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(2,-6,-11)$ and $(4,0,-9)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point of the intersection of the elliptic cone $2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ and the line $x=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in parametric form:\n\n$$\nx=y=\\frac{z+8}{2}=t\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=t \\\\ z=2t-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2{t}^{2} & =(2t-8)^{2} \\\\ 4{t}^{2} & =4{t}^{2}-32t+64 \\\\ 32t & =64 \\\\ t & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solution back into the equations of the line. For $t=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ y=2 \\\\ z=2(2)-8=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(2,2,-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,-1,-10)$  only"] ["text" "$(-2,-2,-12)$   only"] ["text" "$(1,1,-6)$  and $(2,2,-4)$"] ["text" "$(2,2,-4)$ only"] ["text" "$(3,3,-2)$  and $(-1,-1,-10)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94784" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(2,2,-4)$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=z+5$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=2\\sqrt{3}$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $xz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve for $y$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=2\\sqrt{3}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(x-2)}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}(x-2)}{2})}^{2} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{3(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+4}{4}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}-12x+12}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}-8x+16}{4} & =z+5 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+4 & =z+5 \\\\ z & ={x}^{2}-2x-1 \\\\ z & =({x}^{2}-2x+1)-2 \\\\ z & ={(x-1)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $xz$-plane is $(1,-2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-1,2)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(-1,5)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(1,-2)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(1,-5)$ in the $xz$-plane"] ["text" "$(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-73866" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(1,-2)$ in the $xz$-plane"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=24$ and the plane $z+y=6$ onto the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve for $z$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+y & =6\\,⟹\\,z=6-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(6-y)^{2} & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(36-12y+{y}^{2}) & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12y+36 & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12y+36 & =24 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-12{y}^{2}+18 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({y}^{2}-6{y}^{2}+9) & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(y-3)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{6}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{6}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{6}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71027" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{3}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=8$ and the plane $z-y=2$ onto the $xz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve for $y$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-y & =2\\,⟹\\,y=z-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $y$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(z-2)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({z}^{2}-4z+4)+{z}^{2} & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+4 & =8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{z}^{2}-4z+2 & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2({z}^{2}-2z+1) & =6 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2(z-1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{2}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71963" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{3}=1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1$ and the plane $y-2x=2$ onto the $xz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $y.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\ny-2x=2\\,⟹\\,y=2(x+1)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the hyperboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2(x+1))^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-{x}^{2}-2x-1+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x-1+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =-1 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2x+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{({x}^{2}+4x+4)-4}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+2+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=2"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-71975" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{8}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following best describes the projection of the intersection between the elliptic cone $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=(z-2)^{2}$ and the plane $2z-y-6=0$ onto the $xy$-plane?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $xy$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $z.$ First, we solve the equation of the plane for $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2z-y-6=0\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{y}{2}+3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={(\\frac{y}{2}+3-2)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={(\\frac{y}{2}+1)}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+y+1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-y & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}-4y+4}{4}-1 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the equation of a circle of radius $2\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-1,2)$ in the $xy$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51c236476464ecf72b9b7b79980b52702a0261975e837c84372d815d396421f7"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "A circle centered at $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "A circle centered at $(-1,2)$"] ["text" "A hyperbola centered at $(-1,2)$"] ["text" "A hyperbola centered at $(-2,4)$"] ["text" "A parabola centered at $(1,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "q-94953" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "A circle centered at $(-1,2)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/state.json" "file_sha256" "f359a4547fd1f27dd997b47d6af2209a35ea9e1b4db8c35acd0a1632d2b4b5a5"} {"question" "e-5264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-5264.json" "file_sha256" "85c55d8f934f1e0b832a01bc522011e148006748afb0e55f13b7bacb2581a3ed"} {"question" "e-5264" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-5264.json" "file_sha256" "85c55d8f934f1e0b832a01bc522011e148006748afb0e55f13b7bacb2581a3ed"} {"question" "e-12558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-12558.json" "file_sha256" "94751ef4efadb7eab77af48f7abc272dfcc8d905e51ee5b8b3f35350aaddea11"} {"question" "e-12558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/assets/41e6a96cc57d9ce57d5a7bec35586175d9217f92e4dc2bab106db641db9f24ea.png)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y=-1+1=0 \\\\ z=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-12558.json" "file_sha256" "94751ef4efadb7eab77af48f7abc272dfcc8d905e51ee5b8b3f35350aaddea11"} {"question" "e-5618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the projection of the intersection between the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{(x+1)^2}{16}+\\dfrac{y^2}{4}+\\dfrac{z^2}{16}=1$ and the plane $x-z=3$ onto the $yz$-plane." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-5618.json" "file_sha256" "6fe4e34f1d9a3b8bb698928cc1aec37bfc8afb14259ddb052986482246f0fd26"} {"question" "e-5618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/example-5618.json" "file_sha256" "6fe4e34f1d9a3b8bb698928cc1aec37bfc8afb14259ddb052986482246f0fd26"}] "usage" {"e-12558" [] "q-70568" [] "e-5618" [] "e-5264" []} "basis" 2874 "retractions" [[17592186198920 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$ and the line\n\n${x = t; y = 2t,; z = t$ where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$."] [17592186198155 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = 4t; y = 8t,; z = 2t$\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$."] [17592186198155 :question/worked-solution "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{24} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n(4t)^{2} + (8t)^{2} + 4(2t)^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n16t^{2} + 64t^{2} + 16t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n96t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nt^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution\n\n$$\nt = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-2; y = 8(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-4; z = 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-1$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right)$.\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2; y = 8(\\frac{1}{2}) = 4; z = 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1$,\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1)$."] [17592186198003 :question/problem "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}$."] [17592186198003 :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n{x = t - 1; y = t + 1; z = 2t\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= z^{2} \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2} + (t + 1)^{2} &= (2t)^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} - 2t + 1 + t^{2} + 2t + 1 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2t^{2} + 2 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2 &= 2t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nt &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n${x =-1 - 1 =-2; y =-1 + 1 = 0; z = 2(-1) =-2$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right)$.\n- For $t=1$, we have\n${x = 1 - 1 = 0; y = 1 + 1 = 2; z = 2(1) = 2$,\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2)$."] [17592186198156 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$. First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx - z = 3⟹x = z + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(((z + 3) + 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n((z + 4)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z^{2} + 4z + 4) + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2} + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186197756 17592186197757 17592186197758 17592186197759 17592186197760] "immutable_fields" [[17592186197755 :answer-field/correct] [17592186197755 :answer-field/id] [17592186197755 :answer-field/key] [17592186197755 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
